To update `arr[idx]` by `val`, we must update all Fenwick Tree nodes whose ranges *cover* `idx`. We find these nodes by repeatedly adding `idx & -idx` to `idx`. This expression isolates the lowest set bit, which represents the smallest power-of-2 range ending at `idx`. Adding it moves `idx` to the *next* parent node that covers a larger segment including the current one.
```cpp
void update(int idx, int val, int n, vector<int>& bit) {
    for (; idx <= n; idx += idx & -idx) {
        bit[idx] += val;
    }
}
```

To find the prefix sum `sum[1...idx]`, we sum values from Fenwick Tree nodes whose ranges *collectively form* `1...idx`. We find these nodes by repeatedly subtracting `idx & -idx` from `idx`. This expression isolates the lowest set bit. Subtracting it effectively removes the contribution of the current node's range and moves `idx` to the *previous* node that covers the range just before it.
```cpp
int query(int idx, vector<int>& bit) {
    int sum = 0;
    for (; idx > 0; idx -= idx & -idx) {
        sum += bit[idx];
    }
    return sum;
}
```

Initialize the Fenwick Tree `bit` (an array of size $N+1$) with all zeros. Then, for each element `arr[i]` (assuming 1-indexed `arr`), call `update(i, arr[i], N, bit)`. This takes $O(N \log N)$ time.
```cpp
// Assuming arr is 1-indexed std::vector<int>
vector<int> bit(N + 1, 0);
for (int i = 1; i <= N; ++i) {
    update(i, arr[i], N, bit); // Using the update function
}
```
